(function(){'use strict';const{register,table,code,diagram}=window.AcademyContent;
register({id:49,centralQuestion:'Which computation must happen before the user arrives, and which must use the world as it is now?',objective:'Partition training, feature, retrieval and inference architecture by latency, freshness, state, reproducibility and failure consequences.',sections:[
{id:'time-boundaries',title:'Time is an architectural boundary',body:['Training consumes versioned historical data to estimate parameters; inference applies a released artifact to a new request. Training usually tolerates minutes or hours and emphasizes throughput, reproducibility, checkpoints and accelerator efficiency. Interactive inference has a deadline, concurrent callers and a user-visible failure mode. Sharing all resources without quotas can let a training job starve the production service.','Offline computation precomputes features, embeddings, recommendations or batch scores before they are requested. Online computation uses information available at request time. Moving work offline reduces request latency and often cost, but introduces freshness delay and storage. The question is not whether batch is old-fashioned: it is whether the decision changes materially during the update interval.','For daily inventory planning, a nightly batch may be sufficient. For fraud authorization, yesterday’s velocity features can miss a rapid attack. A hybrid design precomputes slow-changing customer history and joins it with recent transaction counts online. Its contract must state event time, freshness bounds, missing-value fallback, and which snapshot produced each prediction.'],table:table('Place work by consequence',['Work','Typical placement','Gain','Constraint'],[['Model training','Offline pipeline','Efficient large jobs','Reproducible data and validation'],['Corpus embeddings','Offline/incremental','Avoid request-time document encoding','Index freshness/version'],['Recent fraud velocity','Online/streaming','Current behavior','Low-latency state and late events'],['Daily recommendations','Batch plus online filter','Cheap broad ranking','Staleness and eligibility'],['Final policy decision','Online when action is immediate','Current authorization/capacity','Deadline and audit']]),check:{question:'What is the cost of moving a feature offline?',answer:'The request becomes faster, but the feature reflects an older snapshot and needs freshness, invalidation and fallback semantics.'}},
{id:'contracts',title:'Hybrid systems need point-in-time contracts',body:['A feature definition includes source events, entity key, window, event-time rule, aggregation, transformation, and availability delay. An offline training join must reconstruct what the online system could know at the original decision time. Joining a completed daily aggregate into a morning prediction exposes future events and creates a model that cannot be reproduced at serving time.','Keep training and serving transformations aligned through shared definitions or parity-tested implementations. Version the feature contract independently from the model. A model expecting a seven-day count cannot silently receive a thirty-day count because the column name stayed the same.','Batch jobs need idempotent output keys, atomic publication and backfill policy. Publish a complete snapshot only after validation; consumers should not read half a table while it is being written. Streaming jobs add watermarks, late-event handling and checkpointed state. Exactly-once processing claims require understanding the source, state store and sink together.'],diagram:diagram('flow','Hybrid prediction architecture',['Versioned historical data','Offline features + training','Registered release','Online recent features','Decision API'],[[0,1],[1,2],[2,4],[3,4]],'Training uses as-of reconstruction; serving checks freshness and compatible feature contracts.'),failure:'Putting batch outputs directly into a live table row by row can expose mixed snapshots. Stage, validate, then atomically switch the published snapshot reference.',check:{question:'Why can a completed daily aggregate leak information into a morning training example?',answer:'It includes events that happened later that day and were unavailable at the morning decision.'}},
{id:'failure-isolation',title:'Separate resource and failure domains',body:['Training and inference may share an artifact registry and platform, yet need distinct quotas, credentials and scaling policies. A failed experiment should not mutate the production alias. A backfill should not overload the online feature store. Separate control paths and bounded data access keep a throughput-oriented job from consuming all latency-sensitive capacity.','Draw dependencies and label each with deadline, consistency, recovery and owner. If the online feature store fails, the product may use a cached value with a stated age bound, a simpler model, human review, or abstention. The fallback must be evaluated under the same business and safety constraints as the primary path.','Architecture decisions should record the maximum acceptable staleness, recovery time, action reversibility and cost per useful decision. These constraints explain why two superficially similar models can require different serving designs.'],example:'A churn campaign acts once a week. Precomputing scores nightly is cheaper and easier to replay than keeping a GPU API warm all week. A final online eligibility check still removes customers who already opted out or renewed.',check:{question:'Which data should remain online in a weekly churn campaign?',answer:'Time-sensitive eligibility and consent checks, even if the expensive propensity score is computed in batch.'}}
],glossary:[['Offline architecture','Computation performed outside the immediate request path.'],['Online architecture','Computation performed within the decision deadline.'],['Feature freshness','Age of the source information represented by a feature.'],['Atomic publication','Making a complete validated snapshot visible as one change.'],['Backfill','Recomputing historical outputs under a controlled version and time range.'],['Resource isolation','Quotas and boundaries that prevent one workload from starving another.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Partition a retail recommendation system',brief:'Ten million products change daily, user actions arrive continuously, and the homepage must respond within 200 ms.',parts:['Place embedding, candidate generation, recent behavior, ranking and eligibility checks.','Define freshness and point-in-time contracts.','Design atomic batch publication and a feature-store fallback.','Explain cost and failure isolation.'],solution:'Embed changed products offline/incrementally and publish a validated index snapshot. Precompute broad candidates; use recent user state and final inventory/consent filters online. Train with as-of features. Cache bounded-age candidates, use a simple popularity fallback, and isolate training quotas from serving. Validate p99 under feature-store slowdown and do not serve unavailable products merely because a batch score exists.'},sources:{core:['azureWellArchitectedAI','mlflowRegistry'],deep:['kubernetesBasics']},quiz:[
{concept:'Offline placement',prompt:'A weekly campaign uses a GPU endpoint idle 99% of the week. Which redesign is most plausible?',answer:0,options:[['Batch scores plus online consent/eligibility checks.','The score can tolerate delay while consent cannot.'],['Remove consent checks.','Eligibility must remain current.'],['Train on each click.','Unnecessary cost and uncontrolled change.'],['Keep all work synchronous.','It preserves avoidable idle cost.']]},
{concept:'Freshness',prompt:'Fraud velocity updates nightly while attacks unfold in minutes. Which constraint is violated?',answer:1,options:[['Model parameter count.','Count is not the issue.'],['Decision-relevant feature freshness.','Recent behavior is absent.'],['Docker layer order.','Packaging does not refresh data.'],['Glossary completeness.','Unrelated to production.']]},
{concept:'Temporal leakage',prompt:'Training a 9 AM prediction uses a full-day transaction count. What fails?',answer:2,options:[['The count is categorical.','It is numeric.'],['The API cannot count.','Serving can count available events.'],['Point-in-time availability.','The feature includes later events.'],['All aggregation is forbidden.','Time-correct aggregation is valid.']]},
{concept:'Atomic publication',prompt:'Consumers read a batch table while half its rows are updated. What should change?',answer:3,options:[['Raise model complexity.','The snapshot is inconsistent.'],['Use a smaller learning rate.','It cannot fix publication.'],['Ignore version IDs.','They are needed.'],['Stage and validate a complete snapshot, then switch its pointer.','Consumers see a coherent version.']]},
{concept:'Isolation',prompt:'A backfill saturates the online store and prediction p99 fails. Which control was missing?',answer:0,options:[['Separate quotas and bounded access for batch and serving.','This protects latency-sensitive work.'],['A new loss function.','The bottleneck is shared infrastructure.'],['More prompt examples.','Unrelated.'],['A longer test set.','It does not constrain load.']]},
{concept:'Fallback',prompt:'A cached risk feature is three days old. Is it automatically a safe fallback?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, cache hits are always safe.','Staleness may change decisions.'],['No; validate an explicit age bound and fallback policy.','Safety depends on the task.'],['No cache may ever be used.','Bounded staleness can be acceptable.'],['Yes, if HTTP returns 200.','Status does not establish semantics.']]},
{concept:'Feature contract',prompt:'A seven-day count becomes a thirty-day count without renaming the column. What must be versioned?',answer:2,options:[['Only the dashboard color.','Irrelevant.'],['Only the replica count.','Capacity does not define meaning.'],['The aggregation/feature contract and compatible model release.','The numerical meaning changed.'],['Nothing because the dtype is unchanged.','Schema types alone do not preserve semantics.']]}
]});
register({id:50,centralQuestion:'Will buying a faster GPU accelerate this model, or leave a more expensive device waiting?',objective:'Compare CPU/GPU execution, accelerator memory, precision, transfer overhead and workload shape; estimate fit and choose hardware from measured bottlenecks.',sections:[
{id:'hardware',title:'Parallel arithmetic is useful only when work can feed it',body:['CPUs favor flexible control flow, low-latency serial work and rich caches. GPUs devote more resources to parallel throughput, especially dense tensor operations. A small tree model or branch-heavy preprocessing can run efficiently on a CPU; a large batched neural network can exploit a GPU. The presence of AI in a system does not make every stage accelerator-friendly.','GPU speedups depend on enough parallel work, compatible kernels, numeric precision, and data movement. For a tiny request, host-to-device copies and launch overhead may exceed compute time. Batching amortizes those costs but adds waiting and memory demand. Benchmark the full request path at realistic concurrency, not only a large synthetic matrix multiply.','Training and inference use memory differently. Training stores weights, activations, gradients and optimizer state; inference generally omits gradients but may carry large activations, workspace and, for autoregressive models, KV cache. A model fitting by parameter bytes alone can still run out of memory at the target batch or context length.'],table:table('Hardware decision',['Dimension','CPU','GPU'],[['Execution strength','Control flow and modest parallelism','Massively parallel tensor arithmetic'],['Small request overhead','Often lower transfer/launch cost','May be underfilled'],['Memory','Large host memory; cache hierarchy','Limited high-bandwidth device memory'],['Scaling concern','Threads, cores, NUMA and memory bandwidth','Batch/shape, VRAM, kernels and transfers'],['Good evidence','End-to-end latency and utilization','Tokens/items per second at SLO and memory headroom']]),check:{question:'Why can a GPU be slower for a tiny neural request?',answer:'Transfer and kernel-launch overhead can dominate the small amount of arithmetic, especially without batching.'}},
{id:'memory',title:'Estimate the memory floor, then measure the peak',body:['Parameter storage is approximately parameter count multiplied by bytes per parameter. A seven-billion-parameter model at two bytes per parameter needs about 14 billion bytes for weights alone, before framework overhead or KV cache. Quantization can reduce weight storage and bandwidth, but calibration, supported kernels and task-quality changes must be evaluated. A smaller storage format does not guarantee proportionally faster execution.','KV cache grows with layers, key/value dimensions, sequence length and concurrent sequences. Longer prompts and outputs increase this state even when the weights are fixed. Training activations grow with batch and network depth; gradient checkpointing trades extra recomputation for memory savings. Optimizer states can add several parameter-sized tensors.','Leave headroom for temporary workspaces, allocator fragmentation and the longest supported input. Test maximum shapes and cancellation behavior. Out-of-memory errors under burst traffic often reflect concurrency admission, not a need to retrain the model.'],example:'A 24 GB device holding roughly 14 GB of weights has less than 10 GB available for everything else. Ten simultaneous long conversations can exhaust the remaining memory even though one short request succeeds.',failure:'Sizing VRAM from the downloaded model-file size ignores runtime precision, cache, activations and workspace. Measure peak allocated/reserved memory at the supported workload envelope.',check:{question:'Why does increasing context length reduce safe LLM concurrency?',answer:'Each active sequence retains more KV state, leaving less device memory for other sequences.'}},
{id:'benchmark',title:'Choose hardware at the service objective',body:['Measure CPU preprocessing, data transfer, queue wait, accelerator compute and response separately. Low GPU utilization can mean an input loader is slow, requests are too small, batching is poor, or the application is serialized. High GPU utilization can be useful compute or inefficient kernels; pair utilization with completed useful work and quality.','Compare hardware configurations using identical model, precision, input-length distribution, batch policy and SLO. Report cost per successful prediction or generated task, cold-start time, p99 and failure behavior. An expensive accelerator may lower cost per item at high load but waste money at sporadic traffic.','The decision can be hybrid: CPU validation and feature assembly, GPU model execution, CPU policy and serialization. Avoid unnecessary copies across boundaries. If a smaller CPU model meets the business quality floor and latency target, its operational simplicity can outweigh a marginal benchmark gain.'],code:code('python','weight_gb = parameters * bytes_per_parameter / 1e9\npeak_estimate_gb = weight_gb + kv_cache_gb + activation_gb + workspace_gb\nassert peak_estimate_gb < device_gb * 0.85',[ 'Decimal GB is used consistently for the estimate.','Non-weight runtime state is explicitly included.','Headroom is a planning assumption that must be replaced by measured peak tests.'],'This avoids admitting a workload solely because its weight file fits.'),check:{question:'What should replace raw GPU utilization as the purchasing objective?',answer:'Useful task throughput and quality at the required latency/reliability, divided by full cost.'}}
],glossary:[['VRAM','Device memory available to GPU weights, state and computation.'],['Kernel launch','Dispatch of a compute operation to an accelerator.'],['KV cache','Stored attention keys and values for active generated sequences.'],['Quantization','Representation of weights/activations with reduced numeric precision.'],['Gradient checkpointing','Saving fewer activations and recomputing them during backpropagation.'],['Memory headroom','Capacity reserved beyond expected use for peaks and runtime overhead.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Select hardware for two workloads',brief:'A branch-heavy tabular API serves 15 QPS; a document model generates long reports in batches. A 7B model uses 16-bit weights.',parts:['Estimate weight memory and list additional memory terms.','Choose CPU/GPU candidates and benchmark stages.','Explain batch/context effects on latency and fit.','Recommend a decision metric and a maximum-shape test.'],solution:'Weights alone require about 14 GB decimal. Add KV cache, activations, workspace and allocator overhead. Test the tabular workload on CPU first; benchmark batched document generation on GPU with realistic lengths. Measure p99, successful tasks/hour, peak VRAM and full cost; cap concurrency and context from peak tests rather than nominal device capacity.'},sources:{core:['pytorchOptim','pytorchNN'],deep:['azureWellArchitectedAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'CPU versus GPU',prompt:'A small tree ensemble spends most time in branch-heavy preprocessing at 5 QPS. What should precede buying a GPU?',answer:1,options:[['Assume all AI is GPU-bound.','The workload contradicts that.'],['Profile the full path and benchmark a CPU baseline.','Hardware follows measured work.'],['Increase prompt length.','No language model is involved.'],['Disable validation.','That removes an important boundary.']]},
{concept:'Weight memory',prompt:'Seven billion parameters use two bytes each. Approximate weight storage alone is?',answer:0,options:[['14 GB decimal.','7e9 × 2 bytes.'],['7 MB.','Orders of magnitude too small.'],['28 TB.','Orders of magnitude too large.'],['2 GB including all caches.','It understates weights and ignores runtime state.']]},
{concept:'KV cache',prompt:'Weights fit, but long concurrent chats cause OOM. Which component is a likely cause?',answer:2,options:[['Only the model filename.','Names use negligible memory.'],['Only HTTP status codes.','They do not consume the relevant state.'],['KV cache and per-sequence runtime memory.','Both scale with active sequence lengths.'],['The number of glossary terms.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Transfer overhead',prompt:'A GPU kernel takes 0.2 ms but transfers and launches take 4 ms. What can improve efficiency?',answer:3,options:[['Quote only kernel latency.','That hides actual cost.'],['Copy inputs more often.','That increases overhead.'],['Remove all deadline limits.','That does not optimize computation.'],['Reduce copies and benchmark bounded batching.','It amortizes fixed overhead while respecting latency.']]},
{concept:'Quantization',prompt:'A 4-bit model file is much smaller. Is a 4× speedup guaranteed versus 16-bit?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, bit count directly determines latency.','Kernel support and bottlenecks matter.'],['No; validate kernels, overhead, quality and end-to-end throughput.','Storage savings do not guarantee speed.'],['Yes, all hardware supports every format equally.','Support differs.'],['No quantized model can be useful.','Many are useful after validation.']]},
{concept:'Training memory',prompt:'Why can training need much more memory than inference for the same weights?',answer:0,options:[['Gradients, optimizer states and saved activations.','Training retains additional tensors.'],['Training never uses weights.','It updates them.'],['Inference always stores more optimizer state.','Inference generally has none.'],['HTTP doubles parameter count.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Hardware economics',prompt:'A GPU has 95% utilization but very low useful tasks/hour. What conclusion is justified?',answer:2,options:[['It is necessarily cost-optimal.','Busy is not equivalent to productive.'],['The model is necessarily accurate.','Utilization says nothing about quality.'],['Inspect kernel efficiency, padding, retries and task completion.','These explain wasted work.'],['Buy identical devices immediately.','That may scale waste.']]}
]});
register({id:51,centralQuestion:'Is the accelerator waiting for arithmetic, bytes, or data that has not arrived?',objective:'Diagnose compute, memory-bandwidth, capacity and I/O bottlenecks using stage timing, arithmetic intensity, overlap and controlled experiments.',sections:[
{id:'bottleneck',title:'The slowest constrained stage sets the useful rate',body:['A pipeline can be compute-bound when arithmetic units are saturated, bandwidth-bound when movement of bytes limits execution, capacity-bound when required state does not fit, or I/O-bound when storage/network/input decoding cannot supply work. These are different mechanisms. Adding compute helps only when computation is the limiting stage.','Arithmetic intensity is operations performed per byte moved. A large matrix multiplication can reuse loaded values and achieve high intensity; repeated small elementwise operations may move many bytes for little arithmetic. The roofline idea bounds attainable performance by the lower of compute capability and memory bandwidth multiplied by intensity. It is a diagnostic model, not a substitute for profiling real kernels.','Capacity and bandwidth are often confused. More VRAM can make a model fit without increasing bytes per second. Faster memory can accelerate a bandwidth-bound kernel without permitting a larger model. Host memory, device memory and storage each have separate capacities and transfer paths.'],table:table('Evidence before intervention',['Constraint','Observed signature','Candidate intervention'],[['Compute','Busy arithmetic units; kernels dominate','Faster device, mixed precision, efficient kernels'],['Bandwidth','Many byte transfers, low arithmetic intensity','Fuse operations, improve reuse, reduce precision'],['Capacity','OOM at certain batch/context sizes','Reduce active state, checkpoint, shard or larger memory'],['Input I/O','Device idle between batches; loader waits','Parallel decode, prefetch, local data cache'],['Network','Distributed workers wait on communication','Locality, larger useful work per transfer, topology changes']]),check:{question:'Will more VRAM necessarily fix a bandwidth-bound kernel?',answer:'No. Capacity and transfer rate are different properties; the kernel may still wait for bytes.'}},
{id:'measure',title:'Profile a steady state and a cold path separately',body:['Time data fetch, decode, augmentation, host-to-device transfer, compute, synchronization and output. Accelerator calls may be asynchronous, so naive wall-clock timing around a launch can measure dispatch rather than completion. Use framework profiling or appropriate synchronization for the measurement, while avoiding synchronization in the production hot path merely to simplify timing.','Warm caches and compiled kernels differ from first-use behavior. Measure both, then state the traffic assumption. Inspect traces across multiple batches and input shapes; one average hides periodic checkpoints, garbage collection or rare long sequences. A stage that overlaps another may not add its full duration to the critical path.','Use controlled experiments: keep model and input fixed, replace the loader with synthetic in-memory data, vary batch size, move data local, or disable one transformation. If synthetic data raises throughput sharply, the model kernel was not the original bottleneck. Change one factor at a time so the result is attributable.'],failure:'Timing only the CPU return from an asynchronous GPU operation can report an impressive number while the device is still working. Benchmark completion, and explain synchronization overhead separately.',check:{question:'What does a large speedup with an in-memory synthetic loader imply?',answer:'The original storage, decoding or input path constrained throughput; purchasing more model compute may not help.'}},
{id:'optimize',title:'Overlap work only when there is independent capacity',body:['Prefetch loads the next batch while the current batch computes. Pinned host memory and asynchronous transfers may help on supported paths, but consume resources and need measurement. More workers can saturate disk or memory and become slower. Bounded buffers prevent prefetch from exhausting RAM.','Operation fusion reduces intermediate memory writes and kernel-launch overhead. Shape bucketing reduces padding waste; data locality avoids repeated network fetches. Distributed training adds collective communication, synchronization and straggler effects. Doubling workers can yield far less than double throughput when communication or input storage becomes dominant.','After optimization, repeat the original end-to-end quality and SLO measurement. Reducing precision may change outputs; caching can introduce staleness; aggressive overlap can increase memory peaks. Report useful items per second, p99 where relevant, resource headroom and cost, together with the controlled experiment that explains the gain.'],example:'Training spends 40 ms decoding and 20 ms computing each batch with no overlap. Perfectly overlapping independent stages could reduce steady-state batch interval toward 40 ms, not 20 ms. Faster compute alone saves little while decode remains dominant.',check:{question:'Why does perfect overlap not make all stage times disappear?',answer:'The slowest required stage still limits the steady-state production rate, and dependencies constrain what can overlap.'}}
],glossary:[['Arithmetic intensity','Operations performed per byte transferred.'],['Memory bandwidth','Rate at which bytes can move through a memory path.'],['Capacity bottleneck','Required live state exceeds available memory.'],['Prefetch','Loading future work before current work completes.'],['Kernel fusion','Combining operations to reduce launches and intermediate memory traffic.'],['Critical path','Dependent sequence of work that determines completion time.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Diagnose a starving training GPU',brief:'GPU utilization is 25%; batches spend 80 ms decoding, 10 ms transferring and 25 ms computing. Storage is remote.',parts:['Draw the measured stages and distinguish overlap from serial time.','Design three controlled experiments.','Select prefetch, locality, worker count or hardware changes from expected evidence.','Define a memory and quality regression check.'],solution:'Test in-memory predecoded batches, local cached data, and a bounded worker/prefetch sweep. If compute-only throughput is high, fix decode/storage before GPU upgrades. Overlap fetch with compute but cap buffers; measure peak host/device memory. Preserve augmentations and data order semantics, and rerun end-to-end training/validation so performance changes do not alter learning unintentionally.'},sources:{core:['pytorchOptim','pytorchCNN'],deep:['azureWellArchitectedAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'I/O diagnosis',prompt:'Replacing remote images with predecoded RAM batches quadruples training throughput. What was likely limiting?',answer:0,options:[['The input/storage/decode path.','Removing it exposed higher compute capacity.'],['Only parameter count.','The model stayed fixed.'],['Model registry aliases.','They do not explain per-batch waits.'],['The loss function must be invalid.','No evidence supports that.']]},
{concept:'Arithmetic intensity',prompt:'A kernel repeatedly reads large tensors for simple elementwise operations. Which constraint is plausible?',answer:2,options:[['Only disk capacity.','The operation is already in memory.'],['No possible bottleneck.','Bytes still must move.'],['Memory bandwidth at low arithmetic intensity.','Few operations are done per byte.'],['Authentication throughput.','Unrelated to the kernel.']]},
{concept:'Asynchronous timing',prompt:'A timer around a GPU launch reports 0.01 ms though results arrive later. What was measured?',answer:1,options:[['Guaranteed full execution.','The work was asynchronous.'],['Likely dispatch rather than completion.','Use proper profiling/synchronization.'],['Model accuracy.','Time is not quality.'],['All network latency.','No such evidence exists.']]},
{concept:'Prefetch',prompt:'Increasing loader workers from 8 to 64 lowers throughput and exhausts RAM. What should change?',answer:3,options:[['Increase to 128.','That may worsen contention.'],['Remove memory monitoring.','It hides the failure.'],['Assume more workers always help.','Shared resources saturate.'],['Sweep bounded workers/buffers against storage and memory limits.','Concurrency must match actual capacity.']]},
{concept:'Overlap',prompt:'Decode takes 40 ms and compute 20 ms per batch. With ideal independent overlap, the interval approaches?',answer:0,options:[['40 ms.','The slower stage sets the rate.'],['0 ms.','Required work remains.'],['20 ms always.','Decode cannot supply that rate.'],['800 ms.','Multiplying times has no basis.']]},
{concept:'Capacity versus bandwidth',prompt:'A larger-memory GPU fits the model but does not accelerate inference. Why is that possible?',answer:2,options:[['Memory size always sets compute speed.','Capacity differs from throughput.'],['The benchmark must be fraudulent.','The result is plausible.'],['The original bottleneck was bandwidth or compute, not fit.','More capacity need not remove it.'],['Inference never uses memory.','It reads weights and state.']]},
{concept:'Optimization regression',prompt:'Fusing and quantizing kernels improves speed. What remains necessary?',answer:1,options:[['Only a screenshot of utilization.','It does not establish correctness.'],['End-to-end quality, numerical, memory and SLO checks.','Optimization can alter outputs and peaks.'],['Deleting the previous release.','Rollback should remain available.'],['Ignoring long inputs.','They often expose peak constraints.']]}
]});
register({id:52,centralQuestion:'Does this workload need its own operating-system boundary or a reproducible process environment?',objective:'Compare virtual machines and containers by kernel sharing, isolation, packaging, startup, resource control and operational trade-offs.',sections:[
{id:'boundaries',title:'A container packages a process; a VM virtualizes a machine',body:['A virtual machine runs a guest operating system on virtualized hardware managed by a hypervisor. Each VM has its own kernel, which provides a strong boundary and supports differing operating systems. A container usually isolates processes while sharing the host kernel, using namespaces for views and cgroups for resource accounting and limits. Container images package user-space dependencies, not an independent kernel.','This distinction explains startup and density. Containers usually start faster and carry less operating-system overhead, which helps replication and deployment. VMs can provide stronger isolation for mutually untrusted tenants and kernel-level requirements, but booting and managing many guest systems costs time and resources. These are tendencies, not universal performance guarantees.','Containers commonly run inside VMs. The VM supplies a tenant or node boundary; containers provide application packaging and orchestration within it. Choosing one does not force abandoning the other. For GPU workloads, host drivers, device access and runtime compatibility still matter even when the application image is identical.'],table:table('VM and container decisions',['Dimension','VM','Container'],[['Kernel','Separate guest kernel','Shared host kernel'],['Package','Guest OS plus application','Application and user-space dependencies'],['Startup/density','Usually heavier/slower','Usually lighter/faster'],['Isolation','Hardware virtualization boundary','Process/kernel mechanisms; configuration matters'],['Portability limit','Virtual hardware/OS compatibility','Host kernel/architecture/driver compatibility'],['Typical combination','Cloud worker node','Model/API processes within the node']]),check:{question:'Why does an identical GPU container not guarantee identical behavior on every host?',answer:'Host architecture, kernel, device drivers and accelerator runtime compatibility remain part of execution.'}},
{id:'security',title:'Isolation weakens when privileges cross the boundary',body:['A privileged container, host filesystem mount, host networking or mounted container-engine socket can grant broad host control. Running as non-root, dropping unnecessary capabilities, restricting filesystem writes, using read-only mounts and applying runtime policy reduce the attack surface. Resource limits protect neighbors but do not by themselves authorize data access.','Image provenance matters: pin base and application images by digest, scan dependencies, track an SBOM where available, and rebuild to patch vulnerabilities. A signed image proves an identity/integrity claim, not that its behavior is harmless. Model artifacts can also contain executable serialization; load only trusted formats and verified origins.','Secrets should be injected at runtime through controlled stores and identities, not baked into layers. Deleting a key in a later image layer may leave it retrievable from an earlier layer. Rotation must revoke old credentials, update consumers and verify recovery rather than only editing a file.'],failure:'Mounting the Docker socket into an otherwise restricted inference container can let it control the host engine. The apparent container boundary no longer represents the actual privilege boundary.',check:{question:'Why does deleting a secret in a later Dockerfile instruction not guarantee removal?',answer:'Earlier image layers can retain the secret; never include it in the build context or layers.'}},
{id:'operations',title:'Reproducibility includes the environment below the image',body:['Record image digest, model digest, configuration, driver/runtime versions and resource assumptions. Reproducibility is layered: a fixed application image can still encounter different CPU instruction support, GPU kernels, scheduling or external data. Distinguish bitwise reproducibility from statistically equivalent model behavior and operational compatibility.','For a trusted internal stateless API, containers on managed worker VMs are often practical. For untrusted customer code, use a stronger sandbox or VM isolation with tightly scoped networking and storage. For a legacy application requiring a particular kernel, a VM may be simpler than forcing it into a shared-kernel environment.','Evaluate patching, startup, memory overhead, device access, observability, tenant isolation, support skills and cost together. Architecture is successful when the chosen boundary matches the actual trust and resource model, not when it maximizes container count.'],example:'A multi-tenant notebook service executes arbitrary user Python. Packaging notebooks in containers is convenient, but a stronger tenant sandbox, restricted egress and per-tenant storage permissions are needed because users control executable code.',check:{question:'When should VM-level isolation receive extra consideration?',answer:'When executing mutually untrusted code, requiring a distinct kernel, or needing a stronger tenant boundary than ordinary shared-kernel containers provide.'}}
],glossary:[['Hypervisor','Layer that presents virtual hardware to guest operating systems.'],['Namespace','Kernel mechanism isolating a process view of resources.'],['Cgroup','Kernel mechanism accounting for and limiting resource use.'],['Image digest','Content-derived immutable image identity.'],['Privileged container','Container granted broad host capabilities.'],['SBOM','Software bill of materials describing packaged components.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Choose isolation for an AI platform',brief:'The platform hosts a trusted inference API, arbitrary customer notebooks, and a GPU training worker.',parts:['Choose VM/container boundaries for each workload.','List dangerous mounts and privileges to prohibit.','Define reproducibility metadata and patch workflow.','Explain one cost-versus-isolation trade-off.'],solution:'Use containers for the trusted API with non-root/read-only restrictions. Put arbitrary notebooks behind stronger tenant sandboxes or VM boundaries with scoped egress/storage. Run GPU containers on compatible driver-managed nodes and record host/runtime versions. Pin digests, scan/rebuild, inject secrets at runtime, and avoid engine sockets. Accept extra overhead where it buys a necessary trust boundary.'},sources:{core:['dockerImages','dockerfile'],deep:['kubernetesPods','azureWellArchitectedAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Kernel boundary',prompt:'Which statement explains the key VM/container distinction?',answer:1,options:[['Every container boots a separate kernel.','Ordinary containers share the host kernel.'],['VMs have guest kernels; containers generally isolate processes on a shared kernel.','This drives isolation and overhead differences.'],['VMs cannot run containers.','They commonly do.'],['Containers eliminate host dependencies.','Kernel and drivers still matter.']]},
{concept:'Secrets in layers',prompt:'An API key is copied into an image and deleted in the next layer. What is the risk?',answer:0,options:[['The earlier layer can still expose it.','Avoid baking secrets into any layer.'],['The key is guaranteed unrecoverable.','Layer history remains.'],['Only model accuracy changes.','This is credential exposure.'],['No rotation is needed.','Exposed credentials should be revoked.']]},
{concept:'Container privilege',prompt:'A service mounts the host Docker socket to restart itself. What new risk appears?',answer:2,options:[['Only a larger image.','The risk is control.'],['Automatic stronger isolation.','The boundary is weakened.'],['Potential control of the host container engine.','The socket is a powerful authority.'],['Guaranteed lower CPU usage.','No such guarantee exists.']]},
{concept:'Untrusted execution',prompt:'Customers execute arbitrary Python in shared infrastructure. What should determine isolation?',answer:3,options:[['Only image download size.','Trust matters more.'],['Only notebook color theme.','Irrelevant.'],['Assume non-root alone fully contains all attacks.','Kernel and runtime risks remain.'],['Threat model, tenant trust, sandbox/VM boundary, egress and storage permissions.','The authority surface must match the risk.']]},
{concept:'GPU compatibility',prompt:'A pinned image works on one GPU node and fails on another. Which dependency may explain it?',answer:1,options:[['Only the registry alias spelling.','The image is pinned.'],['Host driver, architecture or runtime compatibility.','The image does not include the whole machine.'],['The glossary search.','Unrelated.'],['A fixed image guarantees all hardware support.','It does not.']]},
{concept:'Resource control',prompt:'A memory limit prevents one container using all RAM. Does it authorize access to another tenant’s documents?',answer:0,options:[['No; resource control and data authorization are separate.','Both boundaries are needed.'],['Yes, limits imply trust.','They do not.'],['Yes, if the request is fast.','Latency is irrelevant.'],['No container can access storage.','They can under configured permissions.']]},
{concept:'Image integrity',prompt:'A signed image verifies successfully. What has not thereby been proven?',answer:2,options:[['The signed identity/integrity claim.','That is what verification supports.'],['Whether the artifact matches the signed digest.','Verification can establish that.'],['That the software is vulnerability-free or behaviorally safe.','A trusted signer can sign flawed software.'],['Any provenance at all.','Signing provides some provenance evidence.']]}
]});
register({id:53,centralQuestion:'When a node disappears, which controllers notice, replace work, and restore reachable capacity?',objective:'Trace Kubernetes reconciliation through API server, state store, scheduler, controllers, kubelet, Pods, Deployments, Services and Ingress; reason about failure recovery and resource placement.',sections:[
{id:'control-plane',title:'Desired state is stored; controllers work to make it real',body:['The API server accepts authenticated and authorized changes to cluster objects. The cluster state store preserves desired and observed state. Controllers repeatedly compare those states and take corrective actions. A Deployment declares a desired replica count and rollout policy; its controllers manage ReplicaSets and Pods. This reconciliation loop is why operators describe intent instead of manually starting each replacement process.','The scheduler chooses a node for an unscheduled Pod using resource requests and placement constraints. The kubelet on that node asks the container runtime to run the containers and reports status. Resource requests guide placement; limits constrain runtime consumption. A Pod requesting an unavailable GPU remains pending even if several CPU-only nodes are idle.','The control plane and worker data plane fail differently. Existing containers may continue running during a control-plane outage, but scheduling, reconciliation and administrative changes are impaired. A healthy API server does not prove workers, model loading or request routing are healthy.'],diagram:diagram('kubernetes','Desired-state recovery',['API + cluster state','Controllers + scheduler','Worker kubelet/runtime','Pods + model replicas','Service / Ingress'],[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[2,0]],'Controllers replace missing work; node placement, readiness and networking determine whether capacity becomes usable.'),check:{question:'Why can a GPU Pod remain pending on a cluster with spare CPU?',answer:'Its requested accelerator and placement constraints cannot be satisfied by CPU-only capacity.'}},
{id:'network',title:'Stable discovery is separate from replaceable instances',body:['Pods have replaceable network identities. A Service provides a stable discovery/routing abstraction for a selected set of Pods; readiness determines which endpoints should receive traffic. Selectors must match the intended workload labels. A Service with no eligible endpoints can exist perfectly well while serving nothing.','Ingress describes external HTTP routing rules implemented by an Ingress controller. Creating an Ingress resource without a controller does not magically create a working gateway. TLS termination, authentication and rate limiting depend on the deployed ingress/gateway implementation and configuration, not merely the presence of the object.','ConfigMaps supply nonsecret configuration; Secrets are intended for sensitive values but require access control and encryption configuration. Base64 encoding is representation, not encryption. RBAC, workload identity, network policy and secret-store integration each address a different boundary.'],table:table('Object and responsibility',['Object/component','Responsibility','Does not prove'],[['Deployment','Replica/rollout desired state','Model quality'],['Pod','Co-scheduled container unit','Durable identity or storage'],['Service','Stable access to selected endpoints','Correct selector/readiness'],['Ingress + controller','External HTTP routing','Automatic authorization'],['Scheduler','Placement using requests/constraints','Application readiness'],['Kubelet','Node-level execution/status','Business outcome correctness']]),failure:'A Service selector typo produces zero endpoints while Pods look healthy. Diagnose object relationships, not only the individual status badges.',check:{question:'Why is base64 text in a Kubernetes Secret not enough protection?',answer:'Encoding is reversible without a key; access control, transport and at-rest protection still need configuration.'}},
{id:'recovery',title:'Replacement needs capacity, artifacts, health and time',body:['When a worker fails, controllers eventually create replacements, but recovery is not instantaneous. Detection delays, scheduling, image pulls, model downloads, volume attachment and warmup all consume time. If every surviving node is full, the desired replicas remain pending until capacity appears.','Spread replicas across independent nodes or zones when the failure model requires it. Use readiness to exclude unloading or incompatible instances, startup probes for slow initialization, and liveness for genuine stuck processes. A liveness check that fails whenever a shared database is unavailable can restart the entire fleet and make recovery worse.','Practice node loss with realistic model size and registry bandwidth. Verify that the surviving capacity meets the SLO and that replacements can obtain credentials, images, artifacts and state. Kubernetes restores declared infrastructure intent; engineers must declare an intent that actually preserves service.'],example:'Three replicas on one GPU node protect against a process crash but not that node’s failure. Spreading them across nodes changes the failure domain; maintaining spare capacity determines whether replacements can start.',check:{question:'What does a replica count of three fail to tell you about availability?',answer:'Whether replicas share a node/zone, whether spare capacity exists, and how long replacement, loading and readiness take.'}}
],glossary:[['Control plane','Components storing cluster state and coordinating reconciliation.'],['Reconciliation','Repeated comparison and correction of desired versus observed state.'],['Scheduler','Component assigning pending Pods to suitable nodes.'],['Kubelet','Node agent managing container execution and status.'],['Ingress controller','Implementation that realizes declared external routing rules.'],['Failure domain','Set of components likely to fail together.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Trace a failed-node recovery',brief:'Three GPU replicas share one node. An Ingress exists, but its controller is absent; the model takes four minutes to load.',parts:['Draw API, controllers, scheduler, workers, Service and Ingress relationships.','Identify two availability failures before the node is lost.','Design placement, capacity, startup/readiness and artifact recovery.','State the evidence needed to claim the SLO survives node loss.'],solution:'Install/configure an actual ingress controller and verify routing. Spread replicas across independent eligible GPU nodes, reserve N−1 capacity, and account for four-minute warmup. Use startup/readiness correctly and test artifact fetch/credentials under replacement load. Measure end-to-end success and p99 during node loss, not only Deployment desired count.'},sources:{core:['kubernetesBasics','kubernetesDeploy','kubernetesService'],deep:['kubernetesProbes','kubernetesHPA']},quiz:[
{concept:'Scheduler',prompt:'A Pod requests one GPU but all free nodes are CPU-only. What should you expect?',answer:0,options:[['Pending until compatible capacity is available.','Requests constrain placement.'],['The scheduler turns a CPU into a GPU.','It cannot create hardware.'],['Guaranteed execution without the request.','That violates the workload requirement.'],['Automatic model retraining.','Scheduling does not train models.']]},
{concept:'Service endpoints',prompt:'Pods are ready but a Service has no endpoints. What should be checked first?',answer:2,options:[['Training loss.','The problem is routing selection.'],['Prompt temperature.','Unrelated.'],['Service selectors and Pod labels.','Mismatch excludes the Pods.'],['Only the image size.','It does not establish endpoint membership.']]},
{concept:'Ingress',prompt:'An Ingress resource exists, but no ingress controller is installed. Why is external routing absent?',answer:1,options:[['Ingress resources directly run all network code.','A controller implements them.'],['The declared rules have no implementation reconciling them.','The controller is required.'],['Pods cannot serve HTTP.','They can.'],['The model registry must be deleted.','Unrelated and harmful.']]},
{concept:'Control-plane outage',prompt:'The API server is unavailable while worker processes remain alive. Which statement is most accurate?',answer:3,options:[['All existing inference stops instantly.','Workers may continue.'],['All scaling works normally.','Control operations are impaired.'],['Business accuracy automatically improves.','Unrelated.'],['Existing work may continue, but scheduling/reconciliation and changes are impaired.','Control and data planes differ.']]},
{concept:'Failure domains',prompt:'Three replicas run on one node. Which failure is not covered?',answer:0,options:[['Loss of that node.','All three share the same failure domain.'],['Only one container process crash.','Other replicas may survive that.'],['A single replica restart.','Replica redundancy helps.'],['One request validation error.','That is not a node-level failure.']]},
{concept:'Probe design',prompt:'Every liveness probe fails when a shared database slows. What danger follows?',answer:2,options:[['Automatic database repair.','Restarts do not fix the dependency.'],['Guaranteed lower load.','Cold starts may add load.'],['Fleet restart storms that worsen the shared incident.','Liveness should detect internal stuck state.'],['More accurate probabilities.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Secret encoding',prompt:'A Secret contains base64-encoded credentials. Which claim is false?',answer:1,options:[['RBAC still matters.','It controls access.'],['Base64 is sufficient encryption at rest.','It is reversible encoding.'],['Transport protection still matters.','Credentials cross network paths.'],['Secret access should be least privilege.','That limits exposure.']]}
]});
register({id:54,centralQuestion:'If any replica can receive the next request, where must the conversation, job and database state live?',objective:'Separate stateless serving from durable and transient state; design concurrency, consistency, idempotency, storage and recovery for scalable AI services.',sections:[
{id:'state',title:'Stateless means replaceable local memory, not absence of data',body:['A stateless service can handle a request using the request itself and external dependencies without requiring prior local process history. It may cache a model or open database connections; those can be reconstructed. Durable customer records, conversation history, job status and idempotency records need an explicit source of truth. If losing a Pod loses the only copy, the service is stateful in an operationally important way.','Externalizing state lets a router send successive requests to different replicas, but introduces latency, authorization and consistency concerns. A conversation store needs tenant ownership, ordering, version checks and retention. Sending a conversation ID is not proof the caller may read it.','Transient accelerator state, such as KV cache, creates a performance affinity even when durable history is external. Sticky routing can reuse that cache, but overloads popular replicas and must tolerate loss. A fallback should reconstruct state or restart generation rather than treating one GPU process as permanent.'],table:table('State placement',['State','Durability need','Typical owner','Failure handling'],[['Model weights/cache','Reconstructable','Replica + artifact store','Reload pinned artifact'],['Conversation history','Durable with retention','Authorized database','Versioned append/recovery'],['KV cache','Usually transient','Inference scheduler/replica','Recompute or restart safely'],['Job progress','Durable checkpoint','Queue + state store','Resume/idempotent replay'],['Idempotency key/result','Durable for replay window','Transactional store','Return prior result'],['Vector index','Rebuildable but expensive','Versioned storage/database','Replicated snapshot/restore']]),check:{question:'Can a service cache model weights and still be stateless?',answer:'Yes, if the cache is reconstructable and no unique durable user state depends on that process surviving.'}},
{id:'consistency',title:'Concurrent requests can corrupt a correct model’s workflow',body:['Two requests can read the same conversation version and append conflicting updates. Use optimistic concurrency with a version number or a transactional append to detect conflicts. A last-write-wins overwrite can silently erase one user turn. The model may then answer from an incomplete history even though each inference was individually correct.','Retries create duplicate effects unless the action boundary recognizes an idempotency key. Store key, normalized request identity, status and result under a uniqueness constraint. Reusing a key with different content should be rejected. A cache alone may expire or race; durable transactional semantics are needed when money, tickets or notifications are affected.','Queues decouple producers and workers, but common delivery semantics allow redelivery. A worker can complete an external action then crash before acknowledging. Design the sink or action ledger to identify prior execution; merely acknowledging earlier risks losing work instead. Exactly-once effect is a system property across all relevant boundaries.'],failure:'Keeping an in-memory set of processed job IDs works until the worker restarts or a second replica receives the same job. Deduplication state must survive the failure mode it is meant to handle.',check:{question:'Why is queue acknowledgement alone insufficient to guarantee one payment?',answer:'A crash can occur between payment and acknowledgement; redelivery needs a durable idempotent payment/action boundary.'}},
{id:'storage',title:'Stateful infrastructure needs recovery semantics of its own',body:['A persistent volume survives a container restart according to its storage configuration, but durability, replication and backup are separate properties. A StatefulSet gives stable identities and storage associations; it does not automatically make a database consistent, replicated or backed up. Database-specific protocols still determine leader election and recovery.','Define recovery point objective, the tolerable amount of lost data, and recovery time objective, the tolerable restoration delay. Test backups by restoring them. Replication helps availability but can replicate accidental deletion; backup and immutable history address different risks. Place replicas across failure domains consistent with the business promise.','Separate state access from serving compute scaling. Ten times more API replicas can overload the same database connection limit. Pool connections, cache only safe data, apply backpressure, and benchmark the dependency at the intended fleet size. Scaling the front end alone often moves the bottleneck into the state layer.'],diagram:diagram('flow','Replaceable compute with explicit state',['Load balancer','Stateless inference replicas','Authorized history store','Durable job/action ledger','Versioned artifact/index store'],[[0,1],[1,2],[1,3],[1,4]],'Each store has its own consistency, tenancy, capacity and recovery contract.'),check:{question:'Why does replication not replace backups?',answer:'Replication can propagate logical corruption or deletion; a recoverable historical backup protects against those failures.'}},
{id:'design',title:'Choose affinity deliberately and make it disposable',body:['Session affinity can reduce cache misses and preserve local state efficiency, but it should not become an undocumented availability requirement. Bound session duration, detect dead replicas, and plan how another replica reconstructs history. For long generation, separate job identity from the connection so clients can discover status after a disconnect.','Strong consistency is appropriate for authorization changes, financial actions and idempotency decisions. Eventual consistency can be acceptable for analytics or recommendation counters where bounded lag is understood. Do not demand global strong consistency for every metric, or casually accept stale permissions because a database happens to be eventually consistent.','Document state by owner, key, authority, consistency, retention and recovery. This inventory often reveals more architectural risk than a diagram containing only APIs and GPUs.'],example:'An agent creates a support ticket, then the stream disconnects. The client retries with the same action key. A durable ledger returns the existing ticket ID; an in-memory flag on the lost replica would have created a duplicate.',check:{question:'Which state deserves stronger consistency: daily click analytics or a revoked tool permission?',answer:'Revoked tool permission, because stale authorization can permit an action the user no longer authorizes.'}}
],glossary:[['Stateless service','Service whose unique durable state is not tied to a particular process.'],['Optimistic concurrency','Detecting conflicting writes using an expected version.'],['Idempotency ledger','Durable record linking an action key to request identity and result.'],['Session affinity','Routing related requests to the same instance.'],['RPO','Maximum acceptable data loss measured as a recovery point gap.'],['RTO','Maximum targeted time to restore service.'],['StatefulSet','Kubernetes controller providing stable workload identities and storage associations.'],['Eventual consistency','Replicas converge after a period during which reads may differ.']],exercise:{duration:90,title:'Scale a tool-using conversation service',brief:'Two API replicas store conversations in memory. Retries can duplicate ticket creation; one GPU holds each session’s KV cache.',parts:['Inventory durable and reconstructable state.','Design authorized conversation storage and optimistic concurrency.','Implement an idempotency record schema and retry lifecycle.','Plan cache-affinity failure, database connection limits, RPO/RTO and restore testing.','Walk through worker crash after tool success but before acknowledgement.'],solution:'Externalize tenant-scoped conversation history with versioned append. Use a unique action key with request hash/status/result in a durable transactional ledger and require the tool sink to honor the same key. Treat KV cache as disposable acceleration and reconstruct on another replica. Bound connection pools and queue admission. Replicate state across relevant failure domains, retain backups, and test restore against declared RPO/RTO. On redelivery, query the action result instead of issuing another ticket creation.'},sources:{core:['kubernetesPods','kubernetesDeploy','azureWellArchitectedAI'],deep:['fastapi','kubernetesService']},quiz:[
{concept:'Statelessness',prompt:'A replica caches weights but can reload them after loss. Is that unique durable state?',answer:0,options:[['No; the cache is reconstructable from a pinned artifact.','The replica can be replaced.'],['Yes; any memory makes an API permanently stateful.','The key is recoverability and unique state.'],['No service may cache weights.','Caching is normal.'],['Weights must be stored in a browser.','That is unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Conversation concurrency',prompt:'Two turns overwrite each other after reading version 12. Which control detects the conflict?',answer:1,options:[['A larger model.','It does not coordinate writes.'],['Expected-version transactional append/optimistic concurrency.','Only a matching version can advance safely.'],['A lower temperature.','Sampling does not preserve history.'],['A longer timeout only.','It may increase overlap.']]},
{concept:'Idempotency',prompt:'A worker creates a ticket then crashes before acknowledging. How should redelivery avoid duplication?',answer:2,options:[['Always create another ticket.','That duplicates the effect.'],['Assume the first attempt failed.','The external action succeeded.'],['Use a durable action key and retrieve the prior result from the sink/ledger.','The effect is recognized across retries.'],['Keep a local boolean only.','It vanished with the worker.']]},
{concept:'StatefulSet',prompt:'Does a StatefulSet automatically back up a database?',answer:3,options:[['Yes, every Pod has a historical backup.','The controller does not supply that.'],['Yes, stable names imply replicated data.','Identity is not replication.'],['Yes, if replicas exceed one.','Database protocols still matter.'],['No; stable identity/storage is separate from database backup and consistency.','These need explicit design.']]},
{concept:'RPO',prompt:'A business can tolerate at most five minutes of lost writes. Which objective is being specified?',answer:0,options:[['RPO.','It bounds the recovery-point gap.'],['p99 latency.','That measures request duration.'],['RTO only.','RTO measures restoration time.'],['Model recall.','That is a prediction metric.']]},
{concept:'Database scaling',prompt:'API replicas grow from 10 to 100 and each opens 100 database connections. Why might latency collapse?',answer:1,options:[['The database cannot ever serve AI.','It can with capacity planning.'],['Connection and query demand outgrow the shared state layer.','Compute scaling moved the bottleneck.'],['More replicas always reduce load everywhere.','They can amplify dependency demand.'],['The model became underfit.','The symptom is infrastructure contention.']]},
{concept:'Replication',prompt:'An accidental delete is immediately replicated. What protection was missing?',answer:2,options:[['More identical deletes.','That worsens loss.'],['Only a load balancer.','It does not preserve history.'],['Recoverable historical backup/versioning with tested restore.','Replication alone copied the mistake.'],['Higher GPU memory.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Session affinity',prompt:'Sticky routing improves KV reuse but the chosen replica dies. What must the design support?',answer:3,options:[['Permanent loss of the conversation.','Durable history should survive.'],['Authorization bypass on another replica.','Permissions still apply.'],['No failover because stickiness is absolute.','That makes the cache a single point of failure.'],['Reconstructing state or safely restarting generation elsewhere.','Affinity should be disposable.']]},
{concept:'Consistency',prompt:'Which read is least suitable for casually accepting stale data?',answer:0,options:[['Whether a tool permission was revoked.','Stale authorization can permit forbidden action.'],['Yesterday’s aggregate click count.','Bounded lag may be acceptable.'],['A noncritical analytics trend.','It often tolerates eventual consistency.'],['An approximate popularity rank.','The task may accept lag.']]},
{concept:'Action identity',prompt:'A client reuses an idempotency key with different payment content. What should the service do?',answer:1,options:[['Execute both silently.','That defeats the key semantics.'],['Reject the conflicting request identity.','One key must identify one intended action.'],['Ignore the amount field.','That corrupts business meaning.'],['Delete the previous ledger record.','That removes protection.']]}
]});
})();
